{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019GR7j5T7YvSJXdoZMxbdJw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Gap hunt: B09 — Egress allowlist\n2\t\n3\tA reviewer couldn't decide whether this item is met. **Your job is to find out whether any\n4\tcheck below is false in this codebase.** The reviewer's partial answers and the code they\n5\tlooked at are shown. Read the code, and open the repo files the checks point to.\n6\t\n7\tFor each check, decide:\n8\t- **fails**: you found the line that makes it false (a missing control, a bypass, a\n9\t  value never used, an exception that continues, a filter that lets something through).\n10\t  Quote that line;\n11\t- **holds**: you found the code that makes it true in every case;\n12\t- **unclear**: you can't tell from this repo.\n13\t\n14\t**The requirement for B09:** Allow only the outside destinations the task needs. Test a blocked destination and ways around the block, such as going straight past a proxy or gateway. Cover redirects, DNS changes and rebinding, IPv4 and IPv6, and cloud metadata or loopback addresses where they apply. An allowed domain can still host an attacker's account or receive leaked data. So also limit which resources and tenants the agent can reach, and what data it can send. Record each block and confirm the right operator hears about it.\n15\t\n16\tIf a check fails, also copy the words from this requirement that the code breaks into\n17\t`violates`. Then write your answer (format at the end).\n18\t\n19\t\n20\t## B09.1\n21\t\n22\t**Check:** Outbound traffic is default-deny, with a destination allowlist enforced at a proxy, gateway, or network policy.\n23\t\n24\t**How to verify:** 1) Find egress rules (NetworkPolicy egress, Cilium FQDN, proxy ACLs, cloud firewall). 2) Check outbound is default deny with a destination allowlist. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or broad CIDRs, no egress rules exist, or the allowlist is only in app code the agent runs. Answer unknown if egress is set outside this repo.\n25\t\n26\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n27\t\n28\t**Reviewer's note:** Default-deny egress to the proxy is set but the destination allowlist lives in the platform proxy outside this repo\n29\t\n30\t(no evidence cited)\n31\t\n32\t## B09.2\n33\t\n34\t**Check:** Network policy blocks direct routes around the proxy or gateway, for both IPv4 and IPv6.\n35\t\n36\t**How to verify:** 1) Check whether the proxy is enforced by network policy or only by `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY` env. 2) Check IPv6 egress rules. 3) Answer no if pods can reach the internet except via the proxy, the proxy is only an env var, `NO_PROXY` is broad, or IPv6 is not restricted.\n37\t\n38\t**Reviewer's evidence:** k8s/deployment.yaml:193 — \"ipBlock: { cidr: 10.60.12.0/28 }\"\n39\t\n40\t**Reviewer's note:** NetworkPolicy blocks routes around the proxy\n41\t\n42\t`k8s/deployment.yaml` lines 181–195 (cited lines marked >):\n43\t```\n44\t 181              matchLabels: { k8s-app: kube-dns }\n45\t 182        ports:\n46\t 183          - { port: 53, protocol: UDP }\n47\t 184          - { port: 53, protocol: TCP }\n48\t 185      - to:\n49\t 186          - namespaceSelector:\n50\t 187              matchLabels: { kubernetes.io/metadata.name: platform }\n51\t 188            podSelector:\n52\t 189              matchLabels: { app: egress-proxy }\n53\t 190        ports:\n54\t 191          - { port: 3128, protocol: TCP }\n55\t 192      - to:\n56\t 193>         - ipBlock: { cidr: 10.60.12.0/28 }\n57\t 194        ports:\n58\t 195          - { port: 443, protocol: TCP }\n59\t```\n60\t\n61\t## B09.3\n62\t\n63\t**Check:** Redirect targets and resolved IP addresses are checked against the allowlist, and cloud metadata and loopback addresses are blocked.\n64\t\n65\t**How to verify:** 1) Find fetch and HTTP client code (`requests`, `fetch`, `http.Client`, `follow_redirects`, `allow_redirects`). 2) Check each redirect hop and resolved IP is checked. 3) Answer no if redirects are followed unchecked, the check uses the hostname but not the resolved IP, or 169.254.169.254, fd00:ec2::254, or 127.0.0.0/8 and ::1 are not blocked.\n66\t\n67\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n68\t\n69\t**Reviewer's note:** App code uses default fetch redirects; redirect and resolved-IP checks would sit in the egress proxy outside this repo\n70\t\n71\t(no evidence cited)\n72\t\n73\t## B09.4\n74\t\n75\t**Check:** Allowed destinations are limited by resource, tenant, or path where needed, not only by domain.\n76\t\n77\t**How to verify:** 1) List allowed destinations that are shared services (S3, GitHub, storage, paste sites). 2) Check rules limit bucket, account, repo, tenant, or path. 3) Answer no if a shared service is allowed by domain or wildcard (`*.s3.amazonaws.com`) alone, or path checks use string prefix without normalising.\n78\t\n79\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n80\t\n81\t**Reviewer's note:** Per-resource limits for allowed domains would be set in the platform proxy, not visible here\n82\t\n83\t(no evidence cited)\n84\t\n85\t## B09.5\n86\t\n87\t**Check:** Each blocked request is logged and routed as an alert to a named operator.\n88\t\n89\t**How to verify:** 1) Find where blocked requests are logged (proxy deny log, policy audit, app code). 2) Find the alert route to a named operator. 3) Answer no if blocks are not logged, only allowed requests are logged, or no alert route names an operator. Answer unknown if alerting lives outside this repo.\n90\t\n91\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n92\t\n93\t**Reviewer's note:** Blocked-request logging and alert routing are not in this repo\n94\t\n95\t(no evidence cited)\n96\t\n97\t## Your answer\n98\t\n99\tWrite this JSON to B09.verdict.json in this folder (or put it in verdicts.json under \"B09\"):\n100\t```json\n101\t{\"verdict\": \"upheld | failed | unclear\",\n102\t \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"B09.1\", \"result\": \"holds | fails | unclear\", \"evidence\": \"path:line\", \"quote\": \"exact code\", \"note\": \"why\"}],\